# KnowledgeForge UI - React/Vite Project Rules

## Core Principles
- Write modern, performant React code using functional components and hooks
- Prioritize accessibility (a11y) in all UI components
- Implement responsive design for all screen sizes
- Focus on user experience and intuitive interfaces
- Optimize for performance and minimize unnecessary re-renders

## React Best Practices
- Use functional components with React Hooks exclusively
- Prefer const declarations for component definitions
- Use React.memo() for expensive components to prevent unnecessary re-renders
- Implement proper error boundaries for graceful error handling
- Use React.Suspense for code splitting and lazy loading
- Keep component logic focused and break down complex components
- Use custom hooks for reusable stateful logic

## State Management
- Use useState and useReducer for local component state
- Implement useContext for shared state across component trees
- Use useCallback and useMemo to optimize performance-critical functions
- Avoid prop drilling by using context or state management libraries
- Keep state as close to where it's used as possible
- Use useEffect for side effects with proper dependency arrays

## Component Design
- Create reusable, composable components
- Use meaningful prop names with proper TypeScript interfaces
- Implement proper default props and prop validation
- Keep components pure when possible (same input = same output)
- Use compound components pattern for complex UI elements
- Separate presentation components from container components

## Performance Optimization
- Use React.memo() for components that receive stable props
- Implement useCallback for event handlers passed to child components
- Use useMemo for expensive calculations
- Implement proper keys for list items
- Lazy load components and images when appropriate
- Optimize bundle size with code splitting
- Monitor and minimize DOM manipulations

## Accessibility (a11y)
- Use semantic HTML elements (button, nav, main, aside, etc.)
- Implement proper ARIA labels and roles
- Ensure keyboard navigation works for all interactive elements
- Maintain proper color contrast ratios (WCAG AA compliance)
- Add alt text for all images and icons
- Use proper heading hierarchy (h1, h2, h3...)
- Test with screen readers and keyboard-only navigation

## Styling & CSS
- Use CSS modules or styled-components for component-scoped styles
- Follow BEM methodology or similar naming conventions
- Use lowercase for all CSS class names (e.g., .user-profile, .nav-item)
- Implement responsive design with mobile-first approach
- Use CSS custom properties (variables) for consistent theming
- Prefer flexbox and CSS Grid for layouts
- Keep CSS specificity low and avoid !important
- Use semantic class names that describe purpose, not appearance

## Error Handling
- Implement error boundaries to catch and handle React errors gracefully
- Use try-catch blocks for async operations
- Provide meaningful error messages to users
- Log errors to monitoring services for debugging
- Implement fallback UI states for error conditions
- Handle network errors and API failures gracefully

## API Integration
- Use async/await for API calls with proper error handling
- Implement loading states for all async operations
- Cache API responses when appropriate
- Use abort controllers to cancel ongoing requests
- Implement proper retry logic for failed requests
- Handle different HTTP status codes appropriately

## Code Organization
- All components must be placed inside the @components/ folder structure
- Group related components in feature-based folders within @components/
- Use index.js files for clean imports
- Separate business logic from UI components
- Create custom hooks for reusable logic
- Use TypeScript interfaces for prop and state typing
- Keep utility functions in dedicated modules

## File and Directory Management
- **NEVER create empty folders or placeholder files without explicit permission**
- Ask user before creating new directories or major structural changes
- Only create files that contain meaningful implementation
- Avoid creating stub components with TODO comments unless specifically requested
- When creating new components, include proper implementation with tests

## TypeScript (if using)
- Define interfaces for all props and state objects
- Use union types for component variants
- Implement proper generic types for reusable components
- Use type guards for runtime type checking
- Prefer interfaces over types for object shapes
- Use strict TypeScript configuration

## Testing
- Write unit tests for all components using React Testing Library
- Test user interactions and behavior, not implementation details
- Use descriptive test names that explain the expected behavior
- Mock external dependencies and API calls
- Test accessibility with screen reader simulation
- Implement integration tests for critical user flows
- Maintain high test coverage for business logic

## Build & Development
- Use Vite for fast development and optimized builds
- Configure proper environment variables for different stages
- Implement proper code splitting for optimal bundle sizes
- Use source maps for debugging in development
- Configure proper proxy settings for API development
- Use ESLint and Prettier for consistent code formatting

## Code Quality & Formatting Automation
- **CRITICAL**: After any changes to files in the @components/ directory or any other UI files, ALWAYS run the following commands in sequence:
  1. `npm run fix-all` - This runs both prettier formatting and eslint fixes automatically
  2. If there are still linting issues, run `npm run lint:fix` for additional fixes
  3. For manual lint checking, use `npm run lint` to verify all issues are resolved
  4. `npm run test` - Run all tests to ensure changes don't break functionality
- **MANDATORY**: Before completing any task that involves UI changes, run the complete verification process:
  1. `npm run check-all` - Run type-check + lint + format check
  2. `npm run test` - Run all tests to verify functionality
- Available quality scripts:
  - `npm run fix-all`: Runs prettier format + eslint fix (USE THIS FIRST)
  - `npm run format`: Run prettier formatting only
  - `npm run lint:fix`: Run eslint fixes only
  - `npm run check-all`: Run type-check + lint + format check (for code quality verification)
  - `npm run test`: Run all tests (for functionality verification)
  - `npm run dev:fix`: Same as fix-all (alternative command)
- **NEVER** leave code with linting errors, formatting issues, or failing tests
- **ALWAYS** verify both code quality AND test functionality before declaring a task complete
- **TEST REQUIREMENT**: All changes must pass existing tests and new functionality must include appropriate tests

## Security
- Sanitize user input to prevent XSS attacks
- Use environment variables for sensitive configuration
- Implement proper CORS configuration
- Validate API responses before using in UI
- Use HTTPS in production
- Implement proper authentication flows

## User Experience
- Implement proper loading states and skeleton screens
- Provide immediate feedback for user actions
- Use progressive enhancement for better perceived performance
- Implement proper form validation with clear error messages
- Use animations and transitions to guide user attention
- Design for mobile-first and touch interactions

## Browser Compatibility
- Test on major browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge)
- Use proper polyfills for newer JavaScript features
- Implement graceful degradation for older browsers
- Test on different screen sizes and orientations
- Use autoprefixer for CSS vendor prefixes

## Comments & Documentation
- Use JSDoc comments for complex components and functions
- Document props interfaces with clear descriptions
- Keep inline comments for complex business logic
- Maintain README with setup and development instructions
- Document component usage with examples
- Use Storybook for component documentation (if applicable)

## File Naming & Structure
- Use lowercase for all folder names (e.g., user-profile/, components/)
- Use lowercase for all file names (e.g., user-profile.tsx, api-service.ts)
- Use PascalCase for component files (e.g., UserProfile.tsx)
- Use camelCase for utility functions and hooks
- Use kebab-case for CSS files and assets
- Keep component and test files co-located
- Use descriptive file names that reflect functionality
